---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 14: Adding the Forearm - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 14: Adding the Forearm</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">A third servo, a new problem: two stick axes, three joints</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-block;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🦾 Elbow servo joins the arm — and the button becomes a control-pair switch</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Mount and wire a third SG90 as the elbow/forearm joint</li>
                        <li>Solve the "3 joints, 2 axes" problem with a control mode</li>
                        <li>Toggle modes with an edge-detected button</li>
                        <li>Show three joint angles plus the active mode on the OLED</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Key Concepts:</h4>
                    <ul style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                        <li>Control pairs — one input device, selectable targets</li>
                        <li>A state variable as a simple mode machine</li>
                        <li>Why three servos finally demand external power</li>
                        <li>Mechanical chain: shoulder → upper arm → elbow → forearm → gripper</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: The mechanical story -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🦾 Section 1: Where the Forearm Fits</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Our arm has grown one joint per lesson: <strong>base</strong> rotates everything, <strong>shoulder</strong> lifts the upper arm. Now we add the <strong>elbow</strong> — an SG90 at the end of the upper arm that bends the forearm, the last link before the tool:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`shoulder servo horn
        │
   upper arm (~85 mm)
        │
   elbow SG90  <-- NEW: drives the forearm
        │
     forearm
        │
   2-finger gripper  <-- mounts at the tip`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">About that gripper</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    The small two-finger gripper bolts onto the forearm's tip in this lesson — mechanically it rides along, and that's the point: <strong>the elbow's whole job is to position whatever is at the end of the arm</strong>. Powering the gripper's own actuator comes next; for now it travels with the forearm as a visual proof that the end of the chain is where the real work happens.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Pin Map &amp; Wiring</h2>
            
            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Complete robot-arm pin map</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>OLED SDA / SCL:</strong> A4 / A5 (I2C bus)</li>
                    <li><strong>Joystick VRx / VRy / SW:</strong> A0 / A1 / D2</li>
                    <li><strong>Mode button:</strong> A2 — repurposed! Now toggles which joint the Y-axis drives</li>
                    <li><strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo:</strong> pin 9</li>
                    <li><strong>Shoulder servo:</strong> pin 10</li>
                    <li><strong>Elbow servo:</strong> pin 11 — <em>new</em></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Three servos = external power required</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Two SG90s could get away with the Uno's 5V pin under light load. Three cannot — stalled servo spikes will brown-out the Arduino (and corrupt I2C, garbling your OLED). Wire all servo power rails to a <strong>separate 5V supply</strong> (USB power bank or regulated 5V), and connect <strong>grounds together</strong>: external GND and Arduino GND must join at one point or the signals float.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The control problem -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎮 Section 3: Three Joints, Two Axes</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The joystick gives us X and Y. The arm now has base, shoulder, <em>and</em> elbow. Real machines solve this all the time — a radio-controlled excavator has more joints than sticks. The trick: <strong>the stick controls a PAIR of joints, and a button picks which pair</strong>.
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <pre style="margin: 0; color: #4a5568; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.8; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace;"><code>{`mode = SHOULDER (default):        mode = ELBOW:
   X stick -> base servo             X stick -> base servo
   Y stick -> shoulder servo         Y stick -> elbow servo
   
   A2 button click = flip the mode`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The base always gets X — reaching around is the most-used motion. Y then drives whichever vertical joint is "armed". One boolean is the entire state machine:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`bool elbowMode = false;   // false: Y -> shoulder   true: Y -> elbow

// Edge-detect the A2 button once per loop pass
int btnState = digitalRead(modeButton);
if (btnState == LOW && lastBtnState == HIGH) {
    elbowMode = !elbowMode;              // flip the control pair
}
lastBtnState = btnState;

// Y deflection goes to whichever servo is "armed"
if (elbowMode) {
    elbowAngle = constrain(elbowAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                           MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
    elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);
} else {
    shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                              MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #ecfdf5; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #059669;">
                <h4 style="color: #065f46; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🔍 Why this is safe (and clever)</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    The "disarmed" servo simply isn't written — it holds its last angle. When you flip back, it waits where you left it. And the OLED finally earns its keep: the mode text on line 1 tells the pilot which joint is armed before they push the stick. This is exactly how real mode-select buttons work on industrial controls.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Display update -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📺 Section 4: Three Angles on One Screen</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The OLED layout gets a third readout. Base and shoulder share a size-2 row (5 chars each, ~60 px); elbow gets the row below; the debug line squeezes into the last 8 pixels:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    // Title + armed pair (yellow band)
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS  ");
    display.print(elbowMode ? "BASE+ELBOW" : "BASE+SHLDR");

    // Row 1: base & shoulder
    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);

    // Row 2: elbow
    display.setCursor(0, 36);
    display.print("E:");
    printAngle(elbowAngle);

    // Footer: live stick deflection
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 56);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4 style="color: #92400e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">💡 Layout check</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    y=0 title (8px) · y=16 row 1 (16px) · y=36 row 2 (16px, ends 52) · y=56 footer (8px, ends 64). Every pixel budgeted — that's the discipline of pixel displays. If text overlaps, you did the math wrong, not the library.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Complete sketch -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧩 Section 5: The Complete Sketch</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Same architecture, one more servo, one more edge-detected button. Still no <code>delay()</code> in <code>loop()</code>:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`// ============================================================
// Lesson 14: Adding the Forearm (elbow servo + gripper mount)
//   X -> base (pin 9)        always
//   Y -> shoulder (pin 10)   in SHOULDER mode
//   Y -> elbow (pin 11)      in ELBOW mode
//   A2 button -> flip mode   SW click -> home all three
//   OLED -> B/S/E angles + armed pair
// ============================================================

#include <Servo.h>
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

Servo baseServo;
Servo shoulderServo;
Servo elbowServo;

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64
#define OLED_ADDR 0x3C
Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);

// ---- Pins ----
const int joyX       = A0;
const int joyY       = A1;
const int joySW      = 2;
const int modeButton = A2;   // repurposed Lesson-11 button

// ---- Servo state ----
int baseAngle     = 90;
int shoulderAngle = 90;
int elbowAngle    = 90;
const int MIN_ANGLE = 20;
const int MAX_ANGLE = 160;

// ---- Control-pair mode ----
bool elbowMode = false;      // false: Y -> shoulder   true: Y -> elbow

// ---- Joystick calibration ----
int centerX = 512;
int centerY = 512;
const int deadZone = 25;
int lastDX = 0;
int lastDY = 0;

// ---- Independent timers ----
unsigned long lastStepTime    = 0;
unsigned long lastDisplayTime = 0;
const int stepInterval    = 15;
const int displayInterval = 250;
const int maxStep = 4;

int lastSWState  = HIGH;
int lastBtnState = HIGH;

int signOf(int v) {
    return (v > 0) - (v < 0);
}

int stepFor(int deflection) {
    int d = abs(deflection);
    if (d < deadZone) return 0;
    return map(d, deadZone, 512, 1, maxStep);
}

void calibrateJoystick() {
    long sumX = 0, sumY = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
        sumX += analogRead(joyX);
        sumY += analogRead(joyY);
        delay(10);
    }
    centerX = sumX / 50;
    centerY = sumY / 50;
}

void printAngle(int n) {
    if (n < 100) display.print(" ");
    display.print(n);
}

void updateDisplay() {
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 0);
    display.print("ARM STATUS  ");
    display.print(elbowMode ? "BASE+ELBOW" : "BASE+SHLDR");

    display.setTextSize(2);
    display.setCursor(0, 16);
    display.print("B:");
    printAngle(baseAngle);
    display.setCursor(64, 16);
    display.print("S:");
    printAngle(shoulderAngle);
    display.setCursor(0, 36);
    display.print("E:");
    printAngle(elbowAngle);

    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 56);
    display.print("dX:");
    display.print(lastDX);
    display.print("  dY:");
    display.print(lastDY);

    display.display();
}

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
    baseServo.attach(9);
    shoulderServo.attach(10);
    elbowServo.attach(11);
    pinMode(joySW, INPUT_PULLUP);
    pinMode(modeButton, INPUT_PULLUP);
    baseServo.write(baseAngle);
    shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
    elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);

    if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {
        Serial.println("OLED not found - check wiring/address");
        while (true);
    }
    display.clearDisplay();
    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
    display.setTextSize(1);
    display.setCursor(0, 20);
    display.println("Calibrating...");
    display.println("Hands off stick!");
    display.display();

    calibrateJoystick();
    updateDisplay();
}

void loop() {
    // Job 1: timed servo motion (every 15 ms)
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        lastDX = analogRead(joyX) - centerX;
        lastDY = analogRead(joyY) - centerY;

        // X always drives the base
        baseAngle = constrain(baseAngle + signOf(lastDX) * stepFor(lastDX),
                              MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
        baseServo.write(baseAngle);

        // Y drives whichever vertical joint is armed
        if (elbowMode) {
            elbowAngle = constrain(elbowAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                                   MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
            elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);
        } else {
            shoulderAngle = constrain(shoulderAngle + signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY),
                                      MIN_ANGLE, MAX_ANGLE);
            shoulderServo.write(shoulderAngle);
        }
    }

    // Job 2: timed screen refresh (every 250 ms)
    if (millis() - lastDisplayTime >= displayInterval) {
        lastDisplayTime = millis();
        updateDisplay();
    }

    // Job 3: buttons (every pass)
    int swState = digitalRead(joySW);
    if (swState == LOW && lastSWState == HIGH) {
        baseAngle = shoulderAngle = elbowAngle = 90;   // home all
    }
    lastSWState = swState;

    int btnState = digitalRead(modeButton);
    if (btnState == LOW && lastBtnState == HIGH) {
        elbowMode = !elbowMode;                        // flip control pair
        updateDisplay();                               // instant feedback
    }
    lastBtnState = btnState;
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Tuning & challenges -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔧 Section 6: Tuning and Next Steps</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">OLED garbles when elbow moves</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Almost always power, not code. Three servos on USB 5V will brown-out — switch to the external supply and double-check shared ground.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Elbow runs the wrong direction</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Flip its sign: <code>elbowAngle - signOf(lastDY) * stepFor(lastDY)</code>. Mechanical mounting flips directions; code adapts, not hardware.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Forgot which mode you're in</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">That's why the mode lives on line 1 of the screen. Want more? Beep the optional buzzer on pin 8 when the mode flips.</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Arm reaches its limits</h4>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">The forearm adds reach — and new ways to hit the table. Watch the real arm, then shrink <code>MIN/MAX_ANGLE</code> ranges per joint to fit your build.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #0ea5e9;">
                <h4 style="color: #0c4a6e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">🛠️ Put It Together — Challenges</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><strong>Highlight the armed joint:</strong> on the OLED, draw a <code>drawRect()</code> box around whichever readout is armed — B+S row vs E row — instead of relying only on the mode text.</li>
                    <li><strong>Three-way mode:</strong> make the A2 button cycle SHOULDER → ELBOW → BOTH (Y drives both at half step each). What state variable replaces the boolean?</li>
                    <li><strong>Pose memory:</strong> store two 3-servo poses in arrays; SW click toggles between them instead of homing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Gripper prep:</strong> if your gripper has its own servo, pin 5 is free — design the control mode for 4 joints. This is the next lesson's puzzle.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="text-center; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <a href="/programming/quizzes32/lesson14" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 12px 24px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Take Lesson 14 Quiz →
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Navigation -->
        <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-top: 2rem;">
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson13" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    ← Previous: Lesson 13
                </a>
            </div>
            <div>
                <a href="/programming/" style="background-color: #059669; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #047857; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#047857'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#059669'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                    Course Overview
                </a>
            </div>
            <div>
